Permission	Effect on files	Effect on directories
u+s (suid)	File executes as the user that owns the file, not as the user that ran the file.	No effect.
g+s (sgid)	File executes as the group that owns the file.	Files that are created in the directory have a group owner to match the group owner
of the directory.
o+t (sticky)	No effect.	Users with write access to the directory cannot delete or rename files that they do not own.


In Linux, files are normally executed using the permissions of the user who is running them. For example, if you run a backup script, it runs 
with your user privileges. However, there are times when a normal user needs to run a program that requires administrative (root) privileges—like
changing their own password. This is where SUID (Set User ID) and SGID (Set Group ID) come in. They are special permissions that allow a file to 
be executed with the privileges of the file's owner or group, rather than the person running it.

A Real-World Example (Default umask 022)
On most standard Linux distributions, the default umask value is 022. Let’s look at what happens when you create a new directory and a new file under this mask:
Creating a Directory:
Baseline: 777
Subtract umask: 022
Resulting Permission: 755 (drwxr-xr-x)
What this means: You (the owner) get full control (7). Your group and everyone else can view and enter the directory (5), but they cannot modify it.

Creating a File:
Baseline: 666
Subtract umask: 022
Resulting Permission: 644 (-rw-r--r--)
What this means: You can read and write to the file (6). Everyone else can only read it (4). No one can execute it.

